#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pervio-mcp — stdio MCP server exposing the Pervio orchestration layer as tools.

pervio-chat (claude branch, pervio_ctx on) mounts this per-session as service
`perviotools` (tool names mcp__perviotools__<tool>), replacing the prose
pointer: the agent CALLS the orchestration layer instead of re-reading a brief
and shelling out. The seven read-only tools go on the session's --allowedTools
(no approval friction); the four write/spend tools (pervio_start, pervio_rerun,
pervio_schedule, pervio_feedback) are NOT allowlisted, so they fall into the
existing approval flow (phone Allow/Deny) automatically. Machine sub-agents
(PERVIO_WF_ID set) get neither the pointer nor this mount — enforced in
pervio-chat, not here.

Protocol: line-delimited JSON-RPC on stdio, envelope mirrored field-for-field
from pervio-approve (the repo's proven MCP implementation):
  initialize  -> {protocolVersion, capabilities:{tools:{}}, serverInfo}
  tools/list  -> {tools:[{name, description, inputSchema}]}
  tools/call  -> ok:    {content:[{type:"text",text:<json string>}]}
                 error: {content:[{type:"text",text:<message>}], isError:true}
Unknown methods get a JSON-RPC error; notifications/* get no response; a bad
stdin line never exits the process. Unlike pervio-approve there is no FIFO to
block on, so tools/call runs synchronously on the main loop (no threads).

NOTHING one message does may end the loop: a live mount that dies takes all 11
tools out of an in-flight chat with no error the client can render. So every
field is shape-checked before use — non-object message, non-string method,
non-object params (a batch array included) draw -32600 at the message's own id
(id null when it hasn't got a usable one) — and _dispatch additionally runs
inside a per-message boundary that answers -32603 rather than unwinding.

Subprocess layer: tools shell out to the sibling pervio-orchestrate /
pervio-feedback / pervio-sessions (the two session tools, pervio_sessions and
pervio_transcript, are read-only passthroughs over that CLI's projects / list /
transcript verbs), falling back to ~/.pervio/bin when no sibling exists — the
same resolution pervio-chat uses for pervio-approve. 60s timeout (start: 120s),
each child in its OWN session with killpg on timeout (the _call_gated pattern
from pervio-orchestrate) so a hung child leaves no zombies behind.

Dynamic named tools (F3): besides the static tools above, every template file
under ~/.pervio/templates whose WRAPPER opts in with "expose": true (wrapper-only
— a bare-spec template has no wrapper to declare on, so it cannot expose) becomes
one tool "pervio_run_<slug>" (slug: required wrapper field, ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}$).
Its inputSchema is generated from the wrapper's "params" declaration (string/
integer/boolean, required per declaration) plus an ever-present optional "dir"
that overrides spec.dir. The scan happens FRESH on every tools/list AND every
tools/call (the chat can rewrite templates concurrently; resolve-at-use keeps
both views consistent with the same disk state). A template with expose but a
bad slug / bad params declaration is skipped with one stderr line, never
breaking the rest; at most 20 named tools, first-by-filename. tools/call
validates the arguments against the declaration (unknown / missing-required /
type mismatch -> isError; defaults fill in), then launches via orchestrate
start --spec-b64 + --params-b64 and returns {wfId}. Named tools SPEND MODEL
QUOTA and are deliberately NOT on pervio-chat's read-only allowlist — like
pervio_start they ride the approval flow.

Playbooks: a template with kind=="playbook" is conversation methodology, not a
runnable spec. pervio_templates passes its kind/body through verbatim (there is
no spec to strip); pervio_start by its name refuses with "playbook cannot be
started as a workflow — load it into a conversation" (spec_b64 / rerun /
schedule paths surface orchestrate's identical gate); the named-tool scan skips
it — expose:true earns one stderr line, never a tool.

Read-only mount (X1): with PERVIO_MCP_RO in the environment (pervio-chat's codex
branch mounts the server per-invocation with =1), the surface shrinks to the
seven read-only tools, SERVER-enforced: tools/list serves only those seven (the
four write verbs are unlisted and the pervio_run_* scan is skipped entirely —
named tools always spend), and tools/call refuses every write verb and every
pervio_run_*-shaped name with isError "read-only mount: <name> unavailable",
deliberately distinct from the unknown-tool error so a policy denial never
reads as a typo. This gate exists because codex has NO approval mechanism
(no --permission-prompt-tool / allowlist): every mounted tool is callable
un-confirmed, so allowlisting-at-the-client is not a boundary there — spend
must be unreachable AT THE SERVER. Without the env, behavior is byte-for-byte
the full surface described above.
"""
import base64
import binascii
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
WF = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "wf")
TPL = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "templates")
SELF_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))

TIMEOUT = 60.0            # every subprocess call …
START_TIMEOUT = 120.0     # … except `orchestrate start` (spawns the detached runner)
RUN_ID = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9]{12}$")
SLUG_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,31}$")        # wrapper "slug" -> pervio_run_<slug>
PARAM_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,23}$")  # wrapper params[].name
PARAM_TYPES = ("string", "integer", "boolean")
DYN_PREFIX = "pervio_run_"
DYN_CAP = 20              # named tools, first-by-filename; the rest logged + skipped
TRANSCRIPT_CAP = 60000    # pervio_transcript: total serialized chars returned

# Read-only mount (X1). ANY non-empty value flips the gate — fail closed: a
# mounter that sets the env at all clearly wants read-only, and "=0 means
# writable" would be a foot-gun on a server whose whole point is that the
# CLIENT side (codex) cannot gate anything. Unset = the full surface, verbatim.
RO_MODE = bool(os.environ.get("PERVIO_MCP_RO"))

# ---------- the ceiling: a workflow started from a conversation does no more than it ----------
# A turn steered by something it read — a web page, a file in a repository —
# must not come out of these tools with more than its conversation had: not a
# step with more permission, not another directory, not a shell line nobody
# was shown, not a schedule that fires for good (security review 2026-11-18 #2;
# the routes that still went round it, 2026-11-22 #1 and #2).
#
# pervio-chat says which permission mode the conversation runs in
# (PERVIO_MCP_CEILING), its directory (PERVIO_MCP_DIR) and, when the phone is
# asked before a call runs, the approval channel's id (PERVIO_SID) — all
# written into the MCP config, where a turn cannot reach them.
#
# EVERY way a workflow leaves here goes through the same two steps, on the
# workflow AS IT WOULD RUN (the template looked up, a directory override
# applied, parameters filled in, the result validated by `orchestrate resolve`
# — the same code `start` runs):
#
#   1. _hold_to_ceiling.
#      * plan (read-only) starts and schedules nothing.
#      * No step holds more power than the conversation — the scale is
#        _pervio_lib.tier_power's: 0 read (plan, default, read-only), 1 write
#        (acceptEdits, auto, workspace-write), 2 full.
#      * Where nobody is shown the call first (auto, dontAsk), what the MODEL
#        chose stays inside the conversation:
#          - a spec it wrote, or a past run it picked to run again: no shell
#            lines (command steps, setup, verify, a report's actions — they run
#            outside any sandbox), nothing published, and every directory —
#            the one a missing `dir` would fall back to included — inside the
#            conversation's own;
#          - a saved template (by name, or through its named tool): the model
#            chose only WHICH one, perhaps a directory and the declared
#            parameters, so the directory it would run in must be the one the
#            template was saved with, or inside the conversation's. The
#            template's own shell lines and directory are the person's: running
#            their saved workflows is what switching these tools on is for.
#            (A turn that can write a file into ~/.pervio/templates unasked can
#            write ~/.zshrc as well; that is not a line this file can hold.)
#      * bypassPermissions is the person's own "ask me nothing": no ceiling.
#   2. _take_shown, where the phone is asked (default, acceptEdits): the
#      approval tool (pervio-approve) asks this file what the call would run
#      (`pervio-mcp --resolve`), sends THAT to the phone's sheet, and on Allow
#      leaves a one-use token named by its hash. The start goes ahead only if
#      what it is about to run hashes to a token: what was approved is what
#      runs, and a template edited between the tap and the start is refused.
#
# Unset (an older pervio-chat, a direct caller) = no ceiling, as before.
CEILING = os.environ.get("PERVIO_MCP_CEILING", "")
CEILING_DIR = os.environ.get("PERVIO_MCP_DIR", "")
SID = os.environ.get("PERVIO_SID", "")
SHOWN_TTL = 300.0             # seconds an Allow stays good for (the start follows it at once)
_POWER_CLAUDE = {"plan": 0, "default": 0, "acceptEdits": 1, "dontAsk": 1, "auto": 1,
                 "bypassPermissions": 2}
_POWER_CODEX = {"read-only": 0, "workspace-write": 1, "danger-full-access": 2}
_PHONE_IS_ASKED = ("default", "acceptEdits")      # pervio-chat's approval path
MODEL, TEMPLATE = "model", "template"             # who chose what the workflow does


def _ceiling_power():
    """None = no ceiling in force."""
    if not CEILING:
        return None
    p = _POWER_CLAUDE.get(CEILING, 0)             # an unknown mode is held to the least
    return None if p >= 2 else p


def _step_tier(s):
    """(the step's own word for its permission, its power). A word this file
    does not know counts as full power — refused, not waved through."""
    if (s.get("backend") or "claude") == "codex":
        tier = s.get("sandbox", "workspace-write")
        return tier, _POWER_CODEX.get(tier, 2)
    tier = s.get("perm", "default")
    return tier, _POWER_CLAUDE.get(tier, 2)


def _shell_lines(spec):
    """Every place a spec carries a line this Mac's shell would run."""
    out = []
    def take(where, v):
        if isinstance(v, str) and v.strip():
            out.append(where)
    take("setup", spec.get("setup"))
    for s in spec.get("steps") or []:
        if isinstance(s, dict):
            for k in ("command", "setup", "verify"):
                take("step %s: %s" % (s.get("id"), k), s.get(k))
    for a in spec.get("actions") or []:
        if isinstance(a, dict):
            take("actions: run", a.get("run"))
    return out


def _has_dir(d):
    return isinstance(d, str) and bool(d.strip())


def _outside_conversation(d, base=None):
    """Is directory `d` outside the conversation's own? (False when the
    conversation's directory is not known — nothing to measure against.)"""
    if not CEILING_DIR or not _has_dir(d):
        return False
    root = os.path.realpath(os.path.expanduser(CEILING_DIR))
    path = os.path.expanduser(d)
    if not os.path.isabs(path):
        path = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(base) if base else root, path)
    path = os.path.realpath(path)
    return not (path == root or path.startswith(root.rstrip("/") + "/"))


def _unasked():
    """A ceiling is in force and nobody is shown the call before it runs."""
    return _ceiling_power() is not None and CEILING not in _PHONE_IS_ASKED


def _hold_to_ceiling(spec, origin=MODEL, saved_dir=None):
    """Refuse (with words the model can pass on) a workflow that would do more
    than the conversation it is started from. `spec` is the workflow as it
    would run; `origin` says who chose its content (see the rules above), and
    for a saved template `saved_dir` is the directory it was saved with."""
    limit = _ceiling_power()
    if limit is None:
        return
    if CEILING == "plan":
        raise ToolError("this conversation is in plan mode (read-only), so it cannot start or "
                        "schedule a workflow. The person can switch the conversation's mode, "
                        "or start it from the Workflows page in the Pervio app.")
    steps = [s for s in (spec.get("steps") or []) if isinstance(s, dict)]
    over = ["%s (%s)" % (s.get("id"), _step_tier(s)[0]) for s in steps if _step_tier(s)[1] > limit]
    if over:
        raise ToolError(
            "this conversation runs in %s mode; a workflow started from it may not give its steps "
            "more power than that. Over the limit: %s. Allowed here: %s. The person can switch "
            "the conversation's mode, or start the workflow from the Workflows page in the "
            "Pervio app." % (CEILING, ", ".join(over),
                             "perm plan/default or sandbox read-only" if limit == 0
                             else "perm up to acceptEdits, sandbox up to workspace-write"))
    if not _unasked():
        return                                    # the phone's sheet shows the rest (_take_shown)
    top = spec.get("dir")
    if origin == TEMPLATE:
        # The template's own content is the person's; the directory it would
        # run in is the model's only if it is not the one it was saved with
        # (an override, or a parameter that lands in `dir`).
        if top != saved_dir and _outside_conversation(top if _has_dir(top) else "~"):
            raise ToolError("this conversation runs in %s mode and works in %s; it cannot "
                            "start a workflow in another directory (%s)."
                            % (CEILING, CEILING_DIR, top if _has_dir(top) else "~"))
        return
    shell = _shell_lines(spec)
    if shell:
        raise ToolError(
            "this conversation runs in %s mode, where nobody is shown a workflow before it starts; "
            "one that runs shell lines on the Mac (%s) cannot be started from it. Leave them out, "
            "or have the person start it from the Workflows page in the Pervio app."
            % (CEILING, "; ".join(shell[:6])))
    publishing = [str(s.get("id")) for s in steps if s.get("publish")]
    if publishing:
        # A published step's output is written by the Mac itself, outside any
        # sandbox, to a directory the spec names (or to the archive shelf the
        # home page reads) — a file nobody was shown, in a place nobody chose.
        raise ToolError(
            "this conversation runs in %s mode, where nobody is shown a workflow before it starts; "
            "one that publishes (step %s) cannot be started from it. Leave `publish` out, or have "
            "the person start it from the Workflows page in the Pervio app."
            % (CEILING, ", ".join(publishing)))
    base = top if _has_dir(top) else "~"          # what the runner falls back to
    outside = [base] if _outside_conversation(base) else []
    outside += [str(s.get("dir")) for s in steps if _outside_conversation(s.get("dir"), base)]
    if outside:
        raise ToolError(
            "this conversation runs in %s mode and works in %s; a workflow started from it must "
            "stay inside that directory. Outside it: %s."
            % (CEILING, CEILING_DIR, ", ".join(sorted(set(outside)))))


def _sha(spec, extra=None):
    """The name of one exact request: the workflow as it would run, plus (for a
    schedule) the fields saved beside it. Canonical JSON, so the approval tool
    and this server compute the same thing in separate processes."""
    blob = json.dumps({"spec": spec, "extra": extra}, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False,
                      separators=(",", ":"))
    return hashlib.sha256(blob.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


_NOT_SHOWN = ("this conversation asks the person on their phone before a workflow starts, and "
              "what would run now is not what they were shown and allowed%s. Nothing was started. "
              "Call the tool again to ask once more.")


def _take_shown(spec, extra=None):
    """Where the phone is asked: go ahead only on the token the approval tool
    left for exactly this request, and use it up (one Allow, one start)."""
    if _ceiling_power() is None or CEILING not in _PHONE_IS_ASKED:
        return
    if not re.match(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$", SID):
        raise ToolError(_NOT_SHOWN % " (no approval channel)")
    path = os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "chat", SID, "shown", _sha(spec, extra))
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        raise ToolError(_NOT_SHOWN % "")
    try:
        os.unlink(path)
    except OSError:
        raise ToolError(_NOT_SHOWN % "")          # someone else used it first
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or time.time() - st.st_mtime > SHOWN_TTL:
        raise ToolError(_NOT_SHOWN % " (the approval is too old)")


RO_TOOLS = ("pervio_list_runs", "pervio_ledger", "pervio_templates",
            "pervio_archives", "pervio_schedules", "pervio_sessions",
            "pervio_transcript")

# Every tool description ends with this etiquette line (the agent should narrate,
# not dump), and pervio_start additionally carries the spend warning verbatim.
ETIQUETTE = "结果要用对话总结给用户,别只丢 JSON。"
# Every named template tool carries the same spend warning pervio_start does.
SPEND = "启动会消耗真实模型额度;调用前先用一句话向用户确认。"


def _bin(name):
    """Sibling binary first (repo checkout / deployed dir), else ~/.pervio/bin —
    byte-for-byte the BIN_DIR fallback pervio-chat uses for pervio-approve."""
    p = os.path.join(SELF_DIR, name)
    return p if os.path.exists(p) else os.path.join(HOME, ".pervio", "bin", name)


ORCH = _bin("pervio-orchestrate")
FEEDBACK = _bin("pervio-feedback")
SESSIONS = _bin("pervio-sessions")


def _send(obj):
    """One JSON-RPC message to stdout (mirrors pervio-approve's _send; single
    thread here, so no lock needed). Tolerant of a closed pipe."""
    try:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(obj) + "\n")
        sys.stdout.flush()
    except (BrokenPipeError, ValueError):
        pass


class ToolError(Exception):
    """A tool-level failure -> MCP isError result (never a dead server)."""


class _RawText(str):
    """A tool result that is ALREADY the wire text: _handle_call ships it
    verbatim instead of json.dumps-ing it again. Used by pervio_transcript's
    total-size cap, whose cut lands mid-JSON — re-encoding would double-quote
    the payload instead of showing the truncated transcript + marker."""


def _run(cmd, timeout):
    """Run a subprocess in its OWN session; on timeout killpg the whole group
    (pervio-orchestrate's _call_gated pattern — no lingering grandchildren).
    Returns stdout text; raises ToolError on timeout / non-zero exit."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.Popen(cmd, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                             stderr=subprocess.PIPE, start_new_session=True)
    except OSError as e:
        raise ToolError("cannot run %s: %s" % (os.path.basename(cmd[1]), e))
    try:
        out_b, err_b = p.communicate(timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        try:
            os.killpg(os.getpgid(p.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            p.kill()
        try:
            p.communicate(timeout=5)
        except (subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError):
            pass
        raise ToolError("%s timed out (>%ds)" % (os.path.basename(cmd[1]), int(timeout)))
    if p.returncode != 0:
        tail = (err_b or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-300:]
        raise ToolError("%s exit %d: %s" % (os.path.basename(cmd[1]), p.returncode,
                                            tail or "failed"))
    return (out_b or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace")


def _orch_json(args, timeout=TIMEOUT):
    """pervio-orchestrate <args>, stdout parsed as JSON."""
    out = _run([sys.executable, ORCH] + args, timeout)
    try:
        return json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        raise ToolError("unparseable output from pervio-orchestrate %s: %s"
                        % (args[0], out[:200]))


def _int_arg(args, key, default, lo, hi):
    """Integer argument, clamped to [lo, hi]. bool is rejected (int subclass);
    an integral float (some clients send 20.0) is tolerated."""
    v = args.get(key, default)
    if isinstance(v, bool) or not isinstance(v, (int, float)) or v != int(v):
        raise ToolError("%s must be an integer" % key)
    return max(lo, min(int(v), hi))


# ---------- read-only tools (allowlisted by pervio-chat) ----------

def t_list_runs(args):
    limit = _int_arg(args, "limit", 20, 1, 100)
    return _orch_json(["list"])[:limit]         # fields pass through verbatim


def t_ledger(args):
    run_id = args.get("runId")
    if not isinstance(run_id, str) or not RUN_ID.match(run_id):
        raise ToolError("runId must match ^[a-z0-9]{12}$")
    cap = _int_arg(args, "output_chars", 2000, 0, 20000)
    path = os.path.join(WF, run_id, "ledger.json")
    try:
        with open(path, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            led = json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise ToolError("no such run: %s" % run_id)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        raise ToolError("unreadable ledger for %s: %s" % (run_id, str(e)[:200]))
    for s in led.get("steps") or []:
        if not isinstance(s, dict):
            continue
        out = s.get("output")
        if isinstance(out, str) and len(out) > cap:
            s["output"] = out[:cap] + "…[truncated %d chars]" % (len(out) - cap)
        # A failed agent step stores the model's FULL final message in `error`
        # (uncapped upstream, unlike output) — the same cap must apply.
        err = s.get("error")
        if isinstance(err, str) and len(err) > cap:
            s["error"] = err[:cap] + "…[truncated %d chars]" % (len(err) - cap)
    return led


def t_templates(args):
    items = _orch_json(["templates", "list"])
    if args.get("include_spec"):
        return items
    # default: metadata only — the spec bodies are big and rarely needed to browse
    return [{k: v for k, v in it.items() if k != "spec"} if isinstance(it, dict) else it
            for it in items]


def t_archives(args):
    items = _orch_json(["archives", "list"])
    for it in items:
        if not isinstance(it, dict):
            continue
        files = it.get("files") or []
        it["filesTotal"] = len(files)
        it["files"] = files[:10]
    return items


def t_schedules(args):
    return _orch_json(["schedule", "list"])     # verbatim


def _sessions_json(cli_args, timeout=TIMEOUT):
    """pervio-sessions <args>, stdout parsed as JSON (read-only CLI)."""
    out = _run([sys.executable, SESSIONS] + cli_args, timeout)
    try:
        return json.loads(out)
    except ValueError:
        raise ToolError("unparseable output from pervio-sessions %s: %s"
                        % (cli_args[0], out[:200]))


def t_sessions(args):
    d = args.get("dir")
    if d is None:
        return _sessions_json(["projects"])     # projects with sessions, verbatim
    if not isinstance(d, str) or not d.strip():
        raise ToolError("dir must be a non-empty string (a path from pervio_sessions)")
    return _sessions_json(["list", "--dir", d])


def t_transcript(args):
    d = args.get("dir")
    if not isinstance(d, str) or not d.strip():
        raise ToolError("dir is required (the project path from pervio_sessions)")
    sid = args.get("session")
    if not isinstance(sid, str) or not sid.strip():
        raise ToolError("session is required (the session id from pervio_sessions)")
    limit = _int_arg(args, "limit", 40, 1, 200)
    cmd = ["transcript", "--dir", d, "--session", sid, "--limit", str(limit)]
    backend = args.get("backend")
    if backend is not None:
        if not isinstance(backend, str) or not backend.strip():
            raise ToolError("backend must be a non-empty string")
        cmd += ["--backend", backend]
    turns = _sessions_json(cmd)
    # Passthrough with a TOTAL-size cap (ledger truncation style): one giant
    # turn must not blow up the calling conversation's context.
    s = json.dumps(turns, ensure_ascii=False)
    if len(s) > TRANSCRIPT_CAP:
        return _RawText(s[:TRANSCRIPT_CAP]
                        + "…[truncated %d chars]" % (len(s) - TRANSCRIPT_CAP))
    return turns


# ---------- write / spend tools (NOT allowlisted -> approval flow) ----------

def _b64_json(obj):
    """A python object as the base64(JSON) the orchestrate CLI's --b64/--spec-b64
    flags take (start / schedule add / schedule update all share the envelope)."""
    return base64.b64encode(
        json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False).encode("utf-8")).decode("ascii")


def _launch(spec):
    """orchestrate start on a workflow already resolved (_final): the last line
    of stdout parsed to {wfId} — the one launch behind pervio_start,
    pervio_rerun and the named template tools."""
    out = _run([sys.executable, ORCH, "start", "--spec-b64", _b64_json(spec)], START_TIMEOUT)
    try:
        return {"wfId": json.loads(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])["wfId"]}
    except (ValueError, KeyError, IndexError):
        raise ToolError("start did not return a wfId: %s" % out[:200])


def _final(spec, params=None):
    """The workflow as `orchestrate start` would run it: parameters filled in
    (and the leftover-placeholder probe run), the result validated — asked of
    orchestrate itself, so there is one answer to "what would run"."""
    cmd = [sys.executable, ORCH, "resolve", "--spec-b64", _b64_json(spec)]
    if params is not None:
        cmd += ["--params-b64", _b64_json(params)]
    out = _run(cmd, TIMEOUT)
    try:
        final = json.loads(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        raise ToolError("unparseable output from pervio-orchestrate resolve: %s" % out[:200])
    if not isinstance(final, dict):
        raise ToolError("pervio-orchestrate resolve did not return a spec")
    return final


def _spec_from_b64(spec_b64):
    """A spec the model submits. Decoded strictly — padding, alphabet, no stray
    whitespace — and re-encoded from the parsed object before anything else
    reads it: the phone's sheet and the runner are both handed OUR bytes, never
    two readings of the model's (a lenient decoder here used to accept what
    the phone could not read, and the sheet then showed only a size)."""
    if not isinstance(spec_b64, str) or not spec_b64.strip():
        raise ToolError("spec_b64 must be a base64 string")
    try:
        spec = json.loads(base64.b64decode(spec_b64, validate=True).decode("utf-8"))
    except (binascii.Error, ValueError) as e:
        raise ToolError("spec_b64 is not base64(JSON): %s" % str(e)[:200])
    if not isinstance(spec, dict):
        raise ToolError("spec_b64 must decode to a spec JSON object")
    return spec


def _own_spec(spec_b64):
    """The model's own spec, as it would run from THIS conversation: left
    without a directory, it works in the conversation's — not in the home
    directory the runner would otherwise fall back to."""
    spec = _spec_from_b64(spec_b64)
    if _ceiling_power() is not None and CEILING_DIR and not _has_dir(spec.get("dir")):
        spec = dict(spec)
        spec["dir"] = CEILING_DIR
    return _final(spec)


# Each _plan_* answers "what would this call run, and who chose it" without
# running it: (the final spec, MODEL or TEMPLATE, the template's saved dir).
# The tool itself and `--resolve` (the approval tool's question) share them.

def _plan_start(args):
    tpl = args.get("template")
    spec_b64 = args.get("spec_b64")
    if bool(tpl) == bool(spec_b64):
        raise ToolError("give exactly one of template or spec_b64")
    if not tpl:
        return _own_spec(spec_b64), MODEL, None
    if not isinstance(tpl, str):
        raise ToolError("template must be a string")
    items = _orch_json(["templates", "list"])
    # Prefer a startable (non-playbook) entry: a playbook sharing a
    # workflow's name must not shadow it into unstartability. The playbook
    # refusal fires only when ONLY a playbook bears the name.
    named = [it for it in items
             if isinstance(it, dict) and it.get("ok") and it.get("name") == tpl]
    match = next((it for it in named if it.get("kind") != "playbook"),
                 named[0] if named else None)
    if match is None:
        names = [it.get("name") for it in items
                 if isinstance(it, dict) and it.get("ok")]
        raise ToolError("template %r not found; available: %s"
                        % (tpl, ", ".join(names) or "(none)"))
    if match.get("kind") == "playbook":
        # A playbook has a body, not a spec — the same words orchestrate's
        # own gate uses, so both doors refuse with one voice.
        raise ToolError("playbook cannot be started as a workflow — "
                        "load it into a conversation")
    spec = match.get("spec")                # cmd_templates already unwrapped {name,summary,spec}
    if not isinstance(spec, dict):
        raise ToolError("template %r has no usable spec" % tpl)
    saved_dir = spec.get("dir")
    d = args.get("dir")
    if d:
        if not isinstance(d, str):
            raise ToolError("dir must be a string")
        spec = dict(spec)
        spec["dir"] = d                     # orchestrate re-validates (no '..' etc.)
    return _final(spec), TEMPLATE, saved_dir


def _plan_rerun(args):
    run_id = args.get("runId")
    if not isinstance(run_id, str) or not RUN_ID.match(run_id):
        raise ToolError("runId must match ^[a-z0-9]{12}$")
    # `orchestrate spec --wf` returns the spec the run was started from,
    # verbatim; an unknown runId surfaces its "no such workflow" here.
    spec = _orch_json(["spec", "--wf", run_id])
    if not isinstance(spec, dict) or not spec:
        raise ToolError("run %s has no usable spec on disk" % run_id)
    # Which past run to run again is the model's pick, and a past run may have
    # been started with more than this conversation has: held as its own spec.
    return _final(spec), MODEL, None


def _go(plan, extra=None):
    """The one way out: held to the ceiling, shown where the phone is asked."""
    spec, origin, saved_dir = plan
    _hold_to_ceiling(spec, origin, saved_dir)
    _take_shown(spec, extra)
    return spec


def t_start(args):
    return _launch(_go(_plan_start(args)))


def t_rerun(args):
    return _launch(_go(_plan_rerun(args)))


_SCHEDULE_FIELDS = ("action", "id", "time", "days", "keepRuns", "enabled")


def _plan_schedule(args):
    """(action, the final spec or None, the fields the phone's sheet shows beside it)."""
    action = args.get("action")
    if action not in ("update", "pause", "resume", "delete", "create"):
        raise ToolError("action must be one of update|pause|resume|delete|create")
    if args.get("enabled") is not None and not isinstance(args.get("enabled"), bool):
        # 1 is not `True`: it used to slip past "is this switching it on?" here
        # and then be saved as on by the runner.
        raise ToolError("enabled must be true or false")
    if action == "create":
        missing = [k for k in ("spec_b64", "time")
                   if not isinstance(args.get(k), str) or not args.get(k).strip()]
        if missing:
            raise ToolError("create needs %s" % " and ".join(missing))
    spec = None
    if action == "create" or (action == "update" and args.get("spec_b64") is not None):
        spec = _own_spec(args["spec_b64"])
    extra = {k: args.get(k) for k in _SCHEDULE_FIELDS if args.get(k) is not None}
    return action, spec, extra


def t_schedule(args):
    action, spec, extra = _plan_schedule(args)
    if spec is not None:
        _go((spec, MODEL, None), extra)
    if action == "create":
        payload = {"time": args["time"], "spec": spec}
        for k in ("days", "keepRuns"):        # optional knobs pass through; the
            if args.get(k) is not None:       # CLI validates shape/range
                payload[k] = args[k]
        if _unasked():
            # Nobody was shown this before it was saved, and a schedule goes on
            # firing after the conversation is gone: it waits, switched off,
            # for the person to turn it on from the phone.
            payload["enabled"] = False
        made = _orch_json(["schedule", "add", "--b64", _b64_json(payload)])
        if _unasked() and isinstance(made, dict):
            made["note"] = ("saved PAUSED: this conversation runs in %s mode, so the person turns "
                            "the schedule on themselves — Workflows → Schedules in the Pervio app."
                            % CEILING)
        return made
    sid = args.get("id")
    if not isinstance(sid, str) or not sid.strip():
        raise ToolError("%s needs id (the id from pervio_schedules)" % action)
    if action == "delete":
        return _orch_json(["schedule", "remove", "--id", sid])
    turning_on = action == "resume" or (action == "update" and args.get("enabled") is True)
    if turning_on and _unasked():
        raise ToolError("this conversation runs in %s mode and cannot switch a schedule on — the "
                        "person does that themselves: Workflows → Schedules in the Pervio app."
                        % CEILING)
    paused_for_new_content = False
    if action in ("pause", "resume"):
        payload = {"id": sid, "enabled": action == "resume"}
    else:                                     # update: only the given fields move
        payload = {"id": sid}
        if spec is not None:
            payload["spec"] = spec
        for k in ("time", "days", "keepRuns", "enabled"):
            if args.get(k) is not None:
                payload[k] = args[k]
        if len(payload) == 1:
            raise ToolError("update needs at least one of "
                            "time/days/keepRuns/enabled/spec_b64")
        if spec is not None and _unasked():
            # New content under a clock somebody else armed would fire with
            # nobody having seen it: the same update switches the schedule off
            # (the runner applies that before it writes the spec).
            payload["enabled"] = False
            paused_for_new_content = True
    out = _orch_json(["schedule", "update", "--b64", _b64_json(payload)])
    if paused_for_new_content and isinstance(out, dict):
        out["note"] = ("saved PAUSED: this conversation runs in %s mode, so a schedule whose "
                       "workflow it replaced waits for the person to turn it on — Workflows → "
                       "Schedules in the Pervio app." % CEILING)
    return out


def t_feedback(args):
    arxiv_id = args.get("arxiv_id")
    verdict = args.get("verdict")
    if not isinstance(arxiv_id, str) or not arxiv_id.strip():
        raise ToolError("arxiv_id is required")
    if not isinstance(verdict, str) or not verdict.strip():
        raise ToolError("verdict is required")
    cmd = [sys.executable, FEEDBACK, "--id", arxiv_id, "--verdict", verdict]
    note = args.get("note")
    if note:
        cmd += ["--note", str(note)]
    out = _run(cmd, TIMEOUT)                    # its own whitelist validates id/verdict
    try:
        return json.loads(out)                  # {"ok":true,"line":…} passed through
    except ValueError:
        raise ToolError("unparseable output from pervio-feedback: %s" % out[:200])


